<body>

<!-- ===== HEADER ===== -->
<header class="report-header">
  <div class="label">UNODC Crime Trends Survey · Policy Analysis Report</div>
  <h1>Global Intentional Homicide Trends<br>A Multi-Dimensional Analysis (1990–2023)</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">Tracking three decades of homicide victimization across 201 countries — by region, gender, and age — to inform evidence-based public safety policy.</p>
  <div class="header-meta">
    <span>Data Source: UNODC Crime Trends Survey via Kaggle</span>
    <span>·</span>
    <span>22,092 observations · 201 countries/territories</span>
    <span>·</span>
    <span>Reference Date: 2026-04-21</span>
    <span>·</span>
    <span>Audience: Policy Analysts &amp; Public Safety Stakeholders</span>
  </div>
</header>

<!-- ===== KPI DASHBOARD ===== -->
<section class="kpi-section">
  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Global Median Rate</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">2.57</div>
      <div class="kpi-detail">per 100,000 population<br><em>Most recent year per country</em></div>
      <span class="kpi-trend down">↓ vs 3.35 in 1990</span>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Male-to-Female Ratio</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">3.3×</div>
      <div class="kpi-detail">Median M/F rate ratio<br><em>Persistently high since 1990</em></div>
      <span class="kpi-trend neutral">Stable 2.6–5.7×</span>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Highest National Rate</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">76.3</div>
      <div class="kpi-detail">Turks &amp; Caicos Islands (2022)<br><em>per 100,000</em></div>
      <span class="kpi-trend up">Caribbean outlier</span>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Decade-over-Decade</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">−9.6%</div>
      <div class="kpi-detail">Mean rate 2000s → 2010s<br><em>Largest decade drop recorded</em></div>
      <span class="kpi-trend down">Meaningful progress</span>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Reporting Coverage</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">41–81%</div>
      <div class="kpi-detail">Countries reporting per year<br><em>Declining in most recent years</em></div>
      <span class="kpi-trend up">Caution: 2023 = 42%</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===== MAIN CONTENT ===== -->
<main class="main-content">

  <!-- SECTION 1: GLOBAL OVERVIEW -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-number">01</span>
      <h2>Global Homicide Rate Overview</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="narrative">
      <p>Over the three decades from 1990 to 2023, the global median intentional homicide rate has shown a <strong>modest but meaningful decline</strong> — from 3.35 per 100,000 in 1990 to 1.84 per 100,000 in 2023 among reporting countries. The <em>mean</em> rate has been more volatile, reflecting the outsized influence of high-rate countries, but also trends downward in the most recent decade.</p>
      <p>The world is, on balance, becoming somewhat safer from intentional homicide — but progress is uneven, geographically concentrated, and partially obscured by declining reporting coverage in recent years. The 2023 figures draw on only 84 countries, versus 162 in 2008, which introduces a systematic bias: countries that report more consistently tend to be higher-income nations with lower rates.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-title">Global Median &amp; Mean Homicide Rate per 100,000 Population (1990–2023)</div>
      <div class="chart-wrapper">
        <canvas id="globalTrendChart"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-title">Reporting Country Count by Year</div>
        <div class="chart-wrapper">
          <canvas id="coverageChart"></canvas>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-title">Decade-over-Decade Mean Rate Change</div>
        <div class="chart-wrapper">
          <canvas id="decadeChart"></canvas>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="highlight-box">
      <strong>Key finding:</strong> The global mean rate rose marginally from the 1990s (8.27) to the 2000s (8.52), then fell 9.6% in the 2010s (7.70). The 2020s (7.71) have plateaued — suggesting that the easy gains have been captured, and further progress requires targeted interventions.
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 2: REGIONAL & COUNTRY -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-number">02</span>
      <h2>Regional &amp; Country-Level Comparison</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="narrative">
      <p>Geographic disparities in homicide rates are stark. <strong>Latin America &amp; the Caribbean</strong> stands apart from all other regions with a median rate of 14.1 per 100,000 — more than thirteen times the European &amp; Central Asian median of 1.09. <strong>Sub-Saharan Africa</strong> ranks second at 5.4. By contrast, <strong>East Asia &amp; Pacific</strong> and the <strong>North Africa &amp; Middle East</strong> region report the lowest medians after Europe.</p>
      <p>The top-rate countries are concentrated in the Caribbean and Central/South America. Small island states such as Turks and Caicos Islands (76.3), Saint Kitts and Nevis (64.2), and Jamaica (49.4) record rates comparable to active conflict zones. At the other extreme, Singapore, Qatar, and Japan report rates below 0.25 per 100,000.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-title">Regional Median Homicide Rate (Most Recent Year per Country)</div>
      <div class="chart-wrapper">
        <canvas id="regionalChart"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-title">Top 10 Highest Homicide Rate Countries</div>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Country</th><th>Latest Yr</th><th>Rate (per 100k)</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody id="top10Table"></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-title">10 Lowest Homicide Rate Countries (non-zero)</div>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Country</th><th>Latest Yr</th><th>Rate (per 100k)</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody id="bottom10Table"></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-title">Regional Trend: Median Homicide Rate 1990–2023</div>
      <div class="chart-wrapper-tall">
        <canvas id="regionalTrendChart"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 3: GENDER DISPARITY -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-number">03</span>
      <h2>Gender Disparity in Homicide Victimization</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="narrative">
      <p>One of the most consistent findings in this dataset is the profound and persistent gender gap in homicide victimization. <strong>Males are murdered at roughly 3.3 times the rate of females</strong> (median M/F ratio across countries with both sexes reported). This ratio has remained remarkably stable over three decades, fluctuating between approximately 2.6× and 5.7× depending on the year and sample composition.</p>
      <p>The aggregate male rate (median: ~3.6 per 100,000 in recent years) dwarfs the female rate (~1.0 per 100,000). In high-burden countries like Saint Kitts and Nevis, Venezuela, and the US Virgin Islands, male rates can be 15–27× higher than female rates. However, female victimization is not trivial — it likely includes a significant proportion of intimate partner and domestic violence, which may itself be under-reported in countries with weak institutional protections.</p>
    </div>

