      <h2>Age Group Analysis</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="narrative">
      <p>Homicide risk rises sharply at adolescence and peaks in young adulthood. Among males, the highest median rates are found in the <strong>30–44</strong> (4.08), <strong>25–29</strong> (3.99), and <strong>20–24</strong> (3.44) age bands — the years overlapping most heavily with labor market entry, peer network formation, and potential gang or organized crime involvement. The rate declines substantially after age 45 and is lowest in the 10–14 cohort.</p>
      <p>Among females, the pattern is broadly similar but compressed: rates are low across all age groups, peaking modestly in the 30–44 band (1.39). Notably, the 0–9 age band shows elevated relative risk for young children — likely reflecting infanticide and intra-family violence — both for males (0.48) and females (0.37).</p>
      <p>Youth (15–29) are disproportionately victimized across virtually all high-burden countries, underscoring the urgency of prevention programs targeting this cohort.</p>
    </div>

