  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-number">06</span>
      <h2>Data Limitations &amp; Policy Recommendations</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="limitation-card">
      <h3>⚠ Data Limitations</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Unbalanced panel:</strong> Not all countries report data for every year. The sample of reporting countries shifts substantially across time — the 1990s data come from ~94–107 countries; peak coverage reached ~162 countries around 2008; by 2023 only 84 countries reported. All cross-year comparisons must account for this compositional change.</li>
        <li><strong>Declining recent coverage:</strong> The apparent stabilization or slight uptick in mean rates after 2020 may partially reflect a smaller, more heterogeneous reporting pool, not genuine worsening. Interpret post-2020 trends cautiously.</li>
        <li><strong>United Kingdom caveat:</strong> UK data in the UNODC source refer to England and Wales only; Scotland and Northern Ireland may appear as separate entries. Double-counting risk exists.</li>
        <li><strong>Territories vs. sovereign states:</strong> Small territories (Puerto Rico, Hong Kong, Macao, Guam, US Virgin Islands) are reported separately and should not be aggregated with parent-country figures.</li>
        <li><strong>No interpolation:</strong> Missing years were left absent — rows do not exist for years with no data. Analysts computing trends should account for gaps rather than treating adjacent observations as continuous.</li>
        <li><strong>Definitional variation:</strong> "Intentional homicide" definitions can vary slightly between countries and reporting periods, affecting strict comparability.</li>
        <li><strong>Age group sub-categories (non-ALL):</strong> Only male and female sex categories are reported for specific age groups; the "both" aggregate is only available for the ALL group in many countries. Combine male/female medians for age-group analysis.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-card">
      <h3>1. Prioritize Caribbean and Central American Crime Surge</h3>
      <p>The Caribbean sub-region shows the highest rates globally and significant recent deterioration in several island states. International cooperation — including targeted anti-gang and anti-trafficking programs, judicial capacity building, and regional policing intelligence-sharing — should be treated as an urgent priority. Countries such as Haiti, Ecuador, Saint Kitts and Nevis, and Trinidad and Tobago warrant specific monitoring and support frameworks.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-card">
      <h3>2. Invest in Youth Violence Prevention</h3>
      <p>Young males aged 15–44 are disproportionately victimized across all regions. Evidence-based interventions — gang deterrence programs, vocational training, cognitive behavioral therapy, urban redesign — have documented effectiveness in reducing youth homicide. High-burden countries should integrate age-targeted strategies into national crime reduction plans.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-card">
      <h3>3. Strengthen Gender-Disaggregated Reporting</h3>
      <p>While the M/F ratio is consistently high, not all countries report sex-disaggregated data (Afghanistan, for instance, provides only the "both" category for many years). Universal adoption of sex-disaggregated reporting would enable more precise tracking of femicide and intimate partner violence, which may be systematically under-recorded in aggregate figures.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-card">
      <h3>4. Scale Up Data Coverage for Monitoring</h3>
      <p>Reporting coverage below 50% in 2023 severely limits the ability to assess recent global trends. UNODC and international partners should incentivize and support statistical capacity in low-income countries to ensure that global monitoring is not inadvertently biased toward wealthier, safer nations.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-card">
      <h3>5. Learn from Success Stories</h3>
      <p>El Salvador (−94%), Colombia (−67%), Sri Lanka (−73%), and the Philippines (−71%) demonstrate that dramatic, sustained reductions are achievable. Cross-national policy learning — understanding what institutional, economic, and social mechanisms drove these improvements — should be a research and cooperation priority for the international community.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

